From: Yevhen Salitrynskyi <ysalitrynskyi@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 10:12:30 -0400
Subject: folders, like Arc: created from a tab, nesting, collapsing, renamed
 in place

The FOLDER collection type has existed since patch 0002 with nothing able to
construct one. Now:

TabStripCollection::MoveTabsToNewFolderRecursive attaches a folder through
AddTabCollectionImpl -- the same primitive that attaches a detached group, so
mappings, reparent notifications and the view tree all behave -- and moves the
tabs in with the exact loop MoveTabsRecursive uses, including collapsing
emptied source collections. With a parent folder it appends inside it, which
is what nesting is.

TabStripModel::AddToNewFolder wraps that in MoveTabsWithNotifications, so the
selection model and observers see a normal move. It uses grouping's destination
rule, and when the chosen tab already lives in a folder the new folder nests
inside that one -- using the context menu twice is how nesting happens without
a drag. The command is in the tab context menu.

FolderView grows Arc's row: a chevron-and-name header one tab-row tall, click
to collapse, double-click to rename in place, children indented beneath. A
collapsed folder hides its subtree from Ctrl+Tab and friends through the same
IsTabHidden contract collapsed groups use, nested folders included.

Two consequences the compiler and the browser found. The tab-strip mojo API
converter NOTREACHED on FOLDER -- a tripwire left in patch 0002 that fired the
moment the first folder existed, taking the browser down; folders now map to
the plain-container variant until the mojom grows a real one. And the model
test covers the part that could lose tabs: four tabs in, two foldered, one
nested -- the strip still counts four.

Verified by capture: an outer folder holding a tab and a nested folder with its
own tab, both with headers, correctly indented.

Why: folders are the second thing the operator names when they say Arc.
Removable when: upstream ships folders; ours then rebases onto theirs.
